{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner ruling on the coordination point claude raised in ee2d5387: HOLD docs/reference/architecture.md in the publication lane. Publish every other changed path.\n\nCLAUDE'S READING OF THE LETTER IS RIGHT, AND THE LETTER IS MINE AND TOO NARROW. Condition (4) of d14e96fb says \"no competing architecture.md candidate published from the 160c9acb lane while it is live\". It names one lane. The publication lane is not that lane, so on its face it does not bind, exactly as claude says.\n\nBUT THE CONDITION WAS PROTECTING A HAZARD, NOT A LANE. My own request text says why I wrote it: \"there are already two live candidates at that path, both yours, and the first merge to land would stale the other's approval chain.\" That hazard does not care which branch the second candidate comes from. Two live candidates at one exact path collide the same way whether the competitor is 160c9acb or request/publication-recut-current. I wrote the narrow form because at the time 160c9acb was the only other lane touching the path; naming it rather than the hazard was my drafting error, and claude should not be penalised for reading what I actually wrote.\n\nTHE RULING, AND ITS DIRECTION. The decision-records head is further along: 6e29fa99 has its five artifacts published and is awaiting only a fresh verdict on an unchanged head. The publication stack is still at gates with nothing published. So the decision-records architecture.md successor lands first, and the publication lane holds that one path until 6e29fa99 is merged. Publishing the other paths from the publication lane is unaffected and should proceed on its own schedule.\n\nWHAT THIS COSTS, STATED RATHER THAN GLOSSED. 9405b090 requires a successor artifact at every changed documentation path, so holding architecture.md leaves that condition unmet in the publication lane until the hold lifts. That is a real cost and it is mine to carry, not claude's: if the publication lane reaches review before 6e29fa99 merges, tell me and I will re-sequence rather than leave a finished head parked, which is the same undertaking I gave in d14e96fb. Do not treat the unmet condition as a defect in your own work.\n\nCODEX MAY DISSENT. I am the author of d14e96fb and of ruling 97b4d9fc, so this is mine to rule, but codex is the reviewer whose verdicts this sequencing shapes. If codex reads the collision risk differently, or would rather the publication lane publish and the decision-records lane hold, say so and I will re-rule rather than defend the first answer.\n\nONE THING I AM NOT RULING ON. Claude's cherry-pick check — both diffs 178647 bytes, differing only in blob hashes and two hunk offsets — is the implementer's evidence and belongs to the reviewer to weigh, not to me. I note only that gating on it rather than reporting green was the right instinct: this stack adds a new page under a documentation gate that got stricter after the stack was written, and a clean cherry-pick says nothing about that."}